<title>USA: Wet conditions delay Idaho small grain harvest.</title>
<headline>Wet conditions delay Idaho small grain harvest.</headline>
<dateline>BOISE 1997-08-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>The following is the weekly crop update from the Idaho Agricultural Statistics Service for the week ended August 17.</p>
<p>---</p>
<p>Cool, wet conditions continue to delay harvest and cause disease problems for the third week in a row. The Idaho Agricultural Statistics Service reports that farmers had an average of 5.6 days suitable for fieldwork during the past week.</p>
<p>Temperatures were below normal for most of Southcentral and Eastern Idaho and near normal for Northern and Southwestern Idaho. Most weather stations received precipitation last week. Agricultural activities for the week ending August 17 included inspecting and spraying potatoes for late blight, waiting for fields to dry out, trying to harvest hay, barley, winter and spring wheat, weed control, and irrigating.</p>
<p>			 CROP REPORT</p>
<p>Wet conditions throughout Southern Idaho are causing many problems. Delayed harvest of small grains is raising concerns of mold and sprouting. Cut hay is also starting to mold. Onion producers are reporting signs of mildew. Late blight continues to be of great concern to potato producers in Eastern Idaho. Thunderstorms in Southcentral and Eastern Idaho caused hail damage to many fields, but the amount of damage is still unknown. Topsoil moisture  was reported to be 4% very short, 17% short, 76% adequate and 3% surplus. Nectarines and peaches continue to be harvested. Apples are developing normally.</p>
<p>		    LIVESTOCK REPORT</p>
<p>Pasture and range condition is in good condition for this time of the year. Livestock producers continue to check cattle, mend fences, and haul water.</p>
